<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.8.1: Evaluating Expressions Using Mental Math</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02206</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.8.1: Evaluating Expressions Using Mental Math</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8708f47b-2692-4135-a08a-4220aa96e09b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up (10 minutes)</title>
<para>This warm up activity prompts students to recall strategies for multiplying mentally.</para>
<para>Each expression can be evaluated in different ways. For example, \(19 \cdot 21\) can be viewed as \(19 \cdot (20 + 1)\), as \(21 \cdot (20 − 1)\), or as \((20 − 1) \cdot (20 + 1)\), among other ways. Reasoning flexibly about the structure of numerical expressions encourages students to do the same when rewriting quadratic expressions in this lesson and beyond.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Display one problem at a time. Give students quiet time to think individually for each problem and ask them to give a signal when they have an answer and a strategy. Keep all problems displayed throughout the talk. Follow with a whole-class discussion.</para>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for English Language Learners<title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Speaking<title>MLR 8 Discussion Supports: Speaking</title></para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
       <para>Display sentence frames to support students when they explain their strategy. For example, “First, I _____ because . . .” or “I noticed _____ so I . . . .” Some students may benefit from the opportunity to rehearse what they will say with a partner before they share with the whole class.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Design Principle(s): Optimize output (for explanation)</para>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for Students with Disabilities<title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title></para>
    <para>Representation: Internalize Comprehension<title>Representation: Internalize Comprehension</title></para>
   </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
    <para>To support working memory, provide students with sticky notes or mini whiteboards.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Supports accessibility for: Memory; Organization</para>
  </div></div>

<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Finding a product mentally can be done by changing the way you look at a multiplication problem.</para>
<para>For example, \(9 \cdot 39\) can be thought of as \(9 \cdot (40 − 1)\). For some people, this might be easier to calculate mentally since you can imagine the solution as \(360 − 9\), or 351. This method leverages the distributive property of multiplication over addition.</para>
<para>For the following questions, find each product mentally. Then, explain how you changed the way you looked at each multiplication problem to use the distributive property.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>(\(9 \cdot 11\))</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 99
  For example, \(9 \cdot 11\) can be thought of as \(9 \cdot (10 + 1)\). This simplifies to \(90 + 9\), or 99.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>(\(19 \cdot 21\))</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 399
  For example, \(19 \cdot 21\) can be thought of as \(19 \cdot (20 + 1)\). This simplifies to \(380 + 19\), or 399.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>(\(99 \cdot 101\))</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 9999
  For example, \(99 \cdot 101\) can be thought of as \(99 \cdot (100 + 1)\). This simplifies to \(9900 + 99\), or 9999.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>(\(109 \cdot 101\))</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 11,009
  For example, \(109 \cdot 101\) can be thought of as \(109 \cdot (100 + 1)\). This simplifies to \(10,900 + 109\), or 11,009.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Ask students to share their strategies for each problem. Record and display their responses for all to see. To involve more students in the conversation, consider asking:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item>"Who can restate _______’s reasoning in a different way?"</item>
  <item>"Did anyone have the same strategy but would explain it differently?"</item>
  <item>"Did anyone solve the problem in a different way?"</item>
  <item>"Does anyone want to add on to _______’s strategy?"</item>
  <item>"Do you agree or disagree? Why?"</item>
</list></section>
</content>
